= Inductive reasoning =

== Overview ==
'''Inductive reasoning''' is ampliative reasoning in which premises support a conclusion without deductively entailing it.

The conclusion can therefore be false even when the premises are true.

== Forms of induction ==
Inductive reasoning includes:

* generalizing from samples;
* predicting future observations;
* statistical inference;
* reasoning by analogy;
* estimating frequencies or probabilities;
* learning regularities from repeated evidence.

Induction is broader than simple enumeration.

== Strength ==
Inductive arguments can be stronger or weaker depending on sample quality, representativeness, background knowledge, alternative explanations, prior information, and the relation between evidence and conclusion.

Unlike deductive validity, inductive support usually comes in degrees.

== Problem of induction ==
Hume's problem asks how inferences from observed cases to unobserved cases can be justified without circularly assuming that the future will resemble the past.

Different responses use probability, reliability, pragmatism, naturalized epistemology, or other frameworks.

== Probabilistic induction ==
Modern inductive logic often represents evidential support using [[Conditional probability]].

Bayesian approaches combine prior probability and likelihood to produce posterior support.

[[Bayesian inference]] is one formal family of inductive reasoning, not the definition of induction as a whole.

== Induction and abduction ==
[[Abductive reasoning]] explicitly appeals to explanatory considerations.

Induction need not.

The boundary is debated because explanatory and probabilistic considerations often interact.

== Related concepts ==
[[Reasoning]], [[Inference]], [[Probability]], [[Conditional probability]], and [[Bayesian inference]] provide complementary frameworks for ampliative support.

